{
  "result": "I have been thinking about kitchens at six in the morning, when light comes in and makes everything look kind. The kettle clicks, the refrigerator hums, and for a minute the whole world feels manageable. I like that hour because no one expects anything from you yet. You can stand in socks on cold tile and rehearse being the person you hope to be.\n\nYesterday I burned toast and laughed out loud. Somewhere along the way I decided adulthood meant never being surprised by burnt toast, never finding joy in crooked things. But joy hides in crooked things. It hides in mugs, in plants leaning toward windows, in neighbors waving with coffee in hand like a flag of friendship.\n\nI want to pay more attention. Not efficiently, but tenderly. The way rain smells different on concrete than on grass. The way a friend\u2019s voice lifts when they talk about something they love. The way time slows when you are peeling an orange for someone else.\n\nTo ask what a stranger is reading, to learn the name of the tree outside your window, to try a recipe with too many spices just because it sounds brave. The world is bruised and beautiful, and we get this brief ticket to walk through it, tasting and listening.\n\nSo today I will call my mother, plant something, forgive twice. I will let morning be morning, unhurried and gold, and trust that attention, given freely, returns multiplied. That feels like enough to begin again today.",
